  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThere isn't much to discuss in the weather department over the\nshort term with a sprawling mid 1020 mb high building across the\nGreat Lakes region. Tranquil and pleasant conditions are in\nstore today and tonight. Energetic northwest flow aloft will\ncontinue across southern Canada, and another deepening surface\nlow will shift east towards the James Bay through the middle of\nthe week. As this occurs, broad southwesterly flow will \nintensify across our area on Tuesday. Fairly deep mixing into\nthe base of the stronger flow aloft will result in late morning\nand afternoon breezes, with some gusts into the 25-30 mph range\nappearing more likely across northern Illinois. Dewpoints are\nlikely to mix out into the low to mid 40s, yielding 30-40\npercent afternoon RHs. The overlap of the strongest winds\n(north) does not look to coincide with the driest conditions\n(south) at this time. \n\nA \"cold front\" will push through the area early on Wednesday.\nWhile we'll be modestly cold advecting, deep mixing behind the\nboundary will yield similar or even slightly warmer conditions\ncompared to Tuesday along with some lingering northwesterly\nbreezes. \n\nA fast-moving, low-amplitude wave embedded within the expansive\nbelt of cyclonic northwesterly flow is forecast to drift across\nthe area Thursday night into Friday morning. Somewhat ephemeral\nand disorganized f-gen forcing may interact with a plume of\nmodestly steepened mid-level lapse rates resulting in a chance\nfor some sprinkles or light showers in spots on Friday, although\na parched sub-cloud airmass should limit greatly precipitation \ncoverage. \n\nNext weekend currently looks to remain precipitation-free and\nwarm as southwesterly flow delivers near +15 to +20 C 850 mb \ntemperatures to the region which may result in a smattering of\nlow to mid 80 degree high temperatures in spots.  \n\nCarlaw",
